    text: `## The Cough That Outwaits Treatment

Tuberculosis survives on incompleteness. Cure demands six months of daily drugs, and the moment a recovering patient feels well and stops, the partially killed bacilli return, now resistant to the very drugs that were working. India answered with [[NTEP]], the National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme, whose engine is [[DOTS]], directly observed treatment: a treatment supporter watches each dose swallowed, converting intention into adherence. The sputum test finds the case, the daily observed dose cures it, and the broken chain of transmission is the epidemic's end.

Clinical Pearl: Cough beyond two weeks demands sputum examination for acid-fast bacilli; that single rule finds most infectious cases while they are still curable. {{The nurse teaches that a diagnosed patient on effective treatment becomes rapidly non-infectious, yet households still reject them, and the community nurse's advocacy against this stigma is itself a transmission-control intervention, because hidden patients are untreated patients.}}

[PAGE]

## A Virus Defeated by Adherence

HIV dismantles the immune system quietly for years, and the treatment that reverses it is purely pharmacological: [[Antiretroviral Therapy]] from [[NACO]], free at government centres, combining three drugs that together suppress viral replication below detectable limits. Suppressed virus restores the immune system and, decisively, eliminates sexual transmission, treatment as prevention.

The entire edifice stands on adherence above 95 percent, one missed day in twenty and resistance begins. The nurse therefore treats every missed-dose conversation as salvage, not scolding, and links the patient to support structures before motivation runs out.

{{{Universal precautions protect the caregiver, not just the patient: every blood and body fluid is treated as potentially infectious, because HIV status is unknown far more often than known.}}}

[PAGE]

## Six Diseases, One Umbrella

The mosquito kills more Indians than any wild animal ever could, carrying a portfolio of diseases unified under [[NVBDCP]]. Malaria, the Plasmodium parasite carried by the female Anopheles biting at night, diagnosed by slide or rapid test and treated with full-course antimalarials. Dengue, the Aedes mosquito biting by day, breeding in stored water in tyres and coolers, causing fever with severe retro-orbital pain and, in its hemorrhagic form, plasma leakage demanding careful fluid management. Chikungunya's crippling joint pains, Japanese encephalitis preying on children in rice-growing districts, filariasis swelling limbs into elephantiasis over years, and kala-azar, the sandfly's burden.

Clinical Pearl: Each vector has its own behavior and therefore its own battle plan: night-biting Anopheles demands insecticide-treated bed nets, while day-biting Aedes demands weekly emptying of stored water. {{A single fogging schedule applied to both is wasted fuel; matching intervention to vector biology is the difference between the programme that works and the programme that performs.}}`
