    text: `## The Diseases That Cannot Spread but Still Kill

The old killers arrived from outside, cholera in the water, plague in the rat. The new killers are manufactured inside the body by years of tobacco, salt, sugar, and sitting. [[Non-Communicable Disease]], cardiovascular disease, cancer, chronic respiratory disease, and diabetes, now causes more deaths in India than all infections combined, and it obeys different rules: no single dramatic outbreak to investigate, only decades of accumulated risk that explodes in a middle-aged stroke.

[[NPCDCS]] organizes the national response across prevention, early detection, and management, with population screening as its spearhead. [[Population-Based Screening]] sends teams to villages and urban wards to test adults over thirty for blood pressure, blood glucose, oral and breast and cervical cancers, catching the hypertensive before the hemorrhage.

Clinical Pearl: {{A screening camp that detects but cannot refer is worse than no camp, because it manufactures anxiety without remedy; every screening activity must be welded to a confirmed referral pathway before the first sample is drawn.}}

[PAGE]

## The Examined Child

The [[School Health Programme]] applies prevention where children are guaranteed to be gathered. Its routine runs on fixed rails: annual medical inspection recording height, weight, vision, hearing, and the skin; immunization catch-up; deworming under the National Deworming Day, February and August; iron-folic acid for adolescent girls; and health education carried into the curriculum itself.

The nurse's inspection converts classroom observation into early detection: the squinting reader referred for refraction before school failure is mislabeled stupidity, the underweight flag inviting anemia workup, the anxious child quietly routed to counseling.

{{{Vision screening is the highest-yield single act: refractive error is the most common correctable cause of childhood underperformance, and a pair of spectacles costs less than a term of failed studies.}}}

[PAGE]

## The School as a Health Machine

A [[Health Promoting School]] goes beyond inspections to redesign the environment: safe drinking water and functioning toilets, midday meals audited for nutrition, tobacco-free campus policy, life-skills education teaching refusal and resilience, and disaster-preparedness drills. The logic is environmental: children do what is available and normal, so the school that normalizes handwashing and stigmatizes tobacco changes behavior more durably than any lecture.

Clinical Pearl: Adolescence is the second window of growth and the first window of risk, tobacco experimentation, early pregnancy, mental distress, and the school health nurse's confidentiality rules decide whether adolescents walk through the clinic door or abandon it. {{The nurse guarantees confidentiality within stated limits, and states those limits honestly, because a promised secret breached once ends all future disclosure from every student who hears of it.}}`
